        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Lithium iron phosphate is the right chemistry for almost every power station buyer. It survives four to six times as many cycles, degrades far more slowly with age and heat, and is significantly harder to push into a thermal event — for a price premium that has largely disappeared.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The nickel-based alternative retains exactly one advantage: it is smaller and lighter for the same capacity. If you carry your power station on your back or into a boat, that is a real reason to accept a shorter life. If it lives in a cupboard and comes out when the lights go off, it is not.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Whichever you own, store it around half charge in a cool place, keep it off the charger between uses, and never charge it below freezing. Those three habits matter more to a pack's working life than the difference between any two well-made units.
          </p>
        </div>


